# Console Command Conventions

Both cards share one command parser, reachable over two transports (RS485
multi-drop and USB CDC). When adding a new command:

- **Update `Console_Help()`** with the new command so `help`/`c:help`/`e:help`
  stays accurate.
- **Work identically over both transports** — commands must go through
  `Console_Exec()` so they work from `Console_Poll()` (RS485) and
  `Console_ExecFromUSB()` (USB CDC) without a separate code path.
- **Respect card-address prefixes** — `c:` (Channel), `e:` (Effect), `*:`/no
  prefix (broadcast). Don't bypass `RS485_IsForMe()`.
- **Reply with `ok` / `err:<code>`** (compact on RS485) — stable tokens
  `syntax`, `range`, `unknown`, `rxdrop`. Never silently clamp a bad value.
- Host tools terminate commands with a **single CR**; cards reply with
  tagged CRLF (`[C]ok\r\n`). Any serial terminal can speak the same ASCII.
- Prefer `<cmd> <value>` where `0` = off (see `scf <kHz>` / `scf 0`) over
  inventing separate `on`/`off` subcommands for a new generator or output
  toggle — it's the established idiom in this codebase.
- **Console polling must stay non-blocking** — no blocking reads or
  `HAL_Delay()` loops in the main loop; follow the `Console_Poll()` pattern
  of draining one byte at a time per iteration. Do not add artificial
  reply holdoffs; production requires Effect `echo off`.
- If a command changes something the operator needs to hear/see the effect
  of, say so in the reply (e.g. remind them which switch to flip) rather than
  silently toggling other state on their behalf.
